WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.517
Cloud GPU workloads can become expensive quickly because they combine high-value accelerator

00:00:04.517 --> 00:00:06.357
capacity with fast-changing engineering demand.

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:09.302
A single experiment, notebook, training job, evaluation run,

00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:12.246
or inference deployment may be reasonable by itself.

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:17.031
The cost grows when many of those activities run without clear ownership, limits,

00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:17.767
or cleanup.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:21.816
Idle time is one of the most common sources of waste.

00:00:21.816 --> 00:00:25.865
A notebook can be open while no useful work is running.

00:00:25.865 --> 00:00:29.913
A worker can wait for data while the GPU is allocated.

00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:33.962
A training run can fail early but leave related resources behind.

00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:37.643
The bill reflects allocated resources, not only productive model progress.

00:00:37.643 --> 00:00:40.587
GPU cost is also more than accelerator time.

00:00:40.587 --> 00:00:43.900
Datasets, checkpoints, logs, artifacts, managed orchestration, monitoring, storage snapshots,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.844
and data movement all affect the final cost.

00:00:46.844 --> 00:00:51.261
Production inference adds another pattern: cost follows demand, latency targets, scaling rules,

00:00:51.261 --> 00:00:52.365
and model efficiency.

00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:56.414
The practical lesson is that cost control is an engineering discipline.

00:00:56.414 --> 00:01:00.463
It should be designed into workflow defaults, permissions, schedules, job queues,

00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:02.671
shutdown policies, monitoring, and review habits.

00:01:02.671 --> 00:01:07.088
Waiting for a surprise bill before adding controls usually means the system

00:01:07.088 --> 00:01:08.928
is already hard to understand.

00:01:08.928 --> 00:01:10.033
Strong ownership matters.

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:15.185
Teams should know who owns a workload, why it exists, what value it supports,

00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:17.026
and when it should stop.

00:01:17.026 --> 00:01:19.970
When technical decisions are connected to financial impact,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.915
engineers can reduce waste without blocking useful work.

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:28.068
The best cost conversations are specific: which job, which owner, which purpose, which limit,

00:01:28.068 --> 00:01:29.540
and which cleanup rule.
